  "title": "Headless build in Brett's AmberX-AXF repo (this worktree, branch claude/asset-conform).",
  "asked": "Headless build in Brett's AmberX-AXF repo (this worktree, branch claude/asset-conform). Read CLAUDE.md and _claude/shared/rules/RULES.md first. Opus, one session, no sub-agents. Brett's rule, exactly: handwritten code is ALLOWED, but ONLY if it conforms to the hot-swappable asset system. Anything the build system makes through the console (templates, boilerplates, plugin generator, Build Pilot, orchestrator/headless builds) is not handwritten by default: it is generated from framework controls + assets. Conforms = every visual value comes from hot-swappable assets/tokens: theme (--fx-* colors/style), scale (typography, density, sizing; compact default, phone-tappable), layout, component-style, menu/toolbar/dialog assets; framework controls instead of raw controls; no hardcoded px/rem/em sizes, spacing, fonts, radii or colors; no var() fallbacks. Swapping any asset must restyle the code with no edits. 1. Write the rule into _claude/shared/rules/RULES.md in plain English. 2. Make templates, boilerplates, the plugin generator, Build Pilot output and build briefs generate conformant code by default. 3. CI check (extend the existing theme/controls checks in framework/cli; wire into .github/workflows/android.yml): FAIL any web CSS/TSX or Kotlin UI, handwritten or generated, that doesn't conform; name file:line and the asset/token to use. Framework core token/asset definition files are allowlisted. 4. Fix every violation (framework/cli/scale-adopt.mjs where it applies) so everything conforms. 5. Run the full local test block from android.yml, lifecycle check, theme/controls checks. All green. 6. Commit; git fetch origin && git merge origin/main; retest; git push origin HEAD:main. If rejected because main moved: fetch, merge, retest, push again until it lands. Also push the branch. 7. Short detailed report (file touched, what broke hot swapping, what it uses now) at _claude/builds/reports/asset-conform.md, committed with the push.",
